uikit: desktop OpenGL through a static EGL, presented with Metal

New CMake option SDL_UIKIT_EGL: a desktop OpenGL backend for the UIKit
video driver on a statically linked EGL (Mesa). SDL_uikitegl.m creates
an EGL surfaceless display and a pbuffer per window that follows the
drawable size; SDL_GL_SwapWindow reads the frame back with
GL_MESA_pack_invert and blits it into the CAMetalLayer drawable of an
SDL Metal view (always display-synced; the swap interval is stored).
Also: GL hooks in SDL_uikitvideo.m, SDL_WindowData.egl, SDL_opengl.h
usable on iOS with desktop GL, SDL_VIDEO_STATIC_ANGLE (static EGL) in
the config template, and UIKit_VideoInit marks the GL library loaded
only for the system OpenGL ES backend.

The readback is asynchronous where GL allows it (pixel pack buffers,
GL_ARB_sync, GL_ARB_map_buffer_range): the frame of swap N goes into one
of two pack buffers behind a fence and is presented at swap N+1. With
GL_ARB_buffer_storage and page-aligned buffers they are persistently
mapped and wrapped as MTLBuffers, so Metal blits straight out of them;
otherwise the frame is copied. Without those features the readback is
synchronous. A frame whose size differs from the drawable (resize in
progress) is not presented. Measured on an iPad Pro (M1): 72-95 fps
with the synchronous readback, 118-120 fps (display cap) with the
asynchronous one.

OpenMW uses plain SDL GL (SDL_WINDOW_OPENGL, SDL_GL_*), so with this
backend it needs no GL changes; Mesa's EGL has no Apple window
platform. Cost: one frame of extra latency and one GPU readback per
frame; rendering straight into the drawable would avoid both.

Upstream: not submitted.

